  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard or waited for the rule to run, so I haven't confirmed the panels render or that the rule fires.\n\n**Data:** The data view `Kibana Sample Data Logs` has web-traffic fields:\n- Request details: `timestamp`, `clientip`, `request`, `url`, `extension`, `bytes`, `host`, `referer`\n- Client and origin: `agent`, `machine.os`, `geo.src`, `geo.dest`\n- Response code: `response`, which is a string, so I filter on `response.keyword`\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-f5322180 Web logs overview\" has five panels:\n- Total requests\n- Requests over time\n- Requests by response code\n- Average bytes by extension\n- Top source countries\n\nYou can open it at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-f5322180-web-logs-overview-cffaf103ddff.\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-f5322180 5xx spike\" is an Elasticsearch query rule.\n- **Schedule:** It checks every minute.\n- **Condition:** It fires when more than 10 documents match `response.keyword: 5*` within the last 5 minutes.\n- **Created disabled:** It was created disabled and I enabled it afterwards. Its ID is `6fb98cdc-315c-4103-9bd2-9e1bb77ac3b9`.\n- **No action attached:** I didn't attach a notification action, so a firing will only show in Kibana's alerts.",
